import type { BlogPost } from './types';
import type { ResearchPost } from './types';

export const november10BlogPosts: readonly BlogPost[] = [{"slug":"prepare-a-court-appointed-fee-application-support-file","title":"Prepare a court-appointed fee application support file","description":"A support file that connects the appointment order, time records, expense receipts, and narrative exhibits before a fee application is signed.","category":"Court fees","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/prepare-a-court-appointed-fee-application-support-file.webp","internalService":"/services/billing-reporting-support","internalSibling":"/blog/set-a-flat-fee-matter-reconciliation-routine-before-the-next-invoice","source":{"label":"U.S. Courts, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/rules-policies/current-rules-practice-procedure/federal-rules-civil-procedure"},"sections":[{"heading":"Start from the appointment order and the fee rules","paragraphs":["A court-appointed fee application begins with the order that created the appointment, not with the time ledger. Read the order for the authorized rate, the covered period, the tasks allowed, and the filing deadline. Confirm whether the court requires a specific form, a supporting brief, or a fee affidavit from the responsible attorney. The support file should open with those requirements so every later document answers a known question.","Copy the governing rule and the local standing order into the file and mark the clauses that change the exhibit set. A district may require time in tenths, separate expense categories, or a statement about prior payments. Write those constraints onto a one-page checklist that the preparer and the reviewer both use. The checklist prevents a complete file from failing on a formatting detail that the court treats as mandatory."]},{"heading":"Build the time and expense register the court expects","paragraphs":["Create one register that lists the date, the timekeeper, the task, the hours, the rate, and the amount for every claimed entry. Add a column for the source record that proves the entry, such as a contemporaneous note, a draft, or a hearing transcript. Keep the register in the court's required order rather than the firm's internal billing order. A reviewer should be able to trace each line from the application to the evidence without opening a second workbook.","Expenses need their own section with a receipt, a business purpose, and a link to the matter. Separate filing fees, travel, experts, and copying so the court can test each category against its rules. Exclude overhead that the appointment order does not allow, even when the firm normally bills it. A clean expense section is easier to defend than a single total with a vague description."]},{"heading":"Reconcile billed time against the source records","paragraphs":["Compare the register with the firm's billing system and with the underlying notes before anyone drafts the narrative. Look for entries that were written after the fact, rounded upward, or duplicated across two tasks. Ask the timekeeper to explain any block of hours that lacks a matching work product. The explanation belongs in the file, because a reviewer may need it when the court asks about a specific day.","Correct errors in the register and keep a short log of what changed and why. Do not silently remove an entry that might still be compensable, because the deletion can create a gap in the chronology. If a block of time cannot be supported, mark it as excluded and show the excluded amount beside the requested total. A documented exclusion is stronger than an unexplained reduction."]},{"heading":"Document the tasks that need narrative support","paragraphs":["Some tasks are clear from a time entry, while others need a sentence that explains why the work was necessary. Identify the research, the conferences, the document review, and the travel that a judge may question without context. Write a short narrative for each category that links the task to the appointment and to the procedural stage. Keep the narrative factual and avoid adjectives that do not add information.","Use the timekeeper's own notes as the source for the narrative and quote them only when the wording is clear. If the note is thin, ask for a supplemental statement before the application is filed. A narrative written from memory months later is weaker than one written while the work is fresh. The support file should show which statements are contemporaneous and which are reconstructions."]},{"heading":"Prepare the exhibit set in filing order","paragraphs":["Assemble the exhibits in the order the court will read them, starting with the order, the application, the fee affidavit, the time register, the expense schedule, and the receipts. Number every page and keep an index that matches the numbers. Use consistent names for the files so the reviewer and the filing clerk can find the right document quickly. A well-indexed file reduces the chance that a chamber's staff will reject it for a missing attachment.","Check the PDF for searchable text, correct page orientation, and readable scans before the filing. Remove draft watermarks and tracked changes that were left in the source documents. Confirm that every exhibit cited in the affidavit is actually attached and that no unredacted client material appears in a public version. The final check should be done by someone who did not assemble the file."]},{"heading":"Run a second review before the application is signed","paragraphs":["Give the reviewer a short brief that names the requested total, the excluded amount, the rate basis, and the three entries most likely to draw a question. Ask the reviewer to check the arithmetic, the dates, and the link between each exhibit and the affidavit. The reviewer should also confirm that the narrative does not overstate what the record shows. A second pass catches the errors that the preparer has stopped seeing.","Record the reviewer's comments and the resolution for each one in the file. If the reviewer disagrees with an entry, resolve it before the signature rather than after the filing. Keep the signed version separate from the working version so the record shows what was submitted. The review log is useful if the court later asks how the firm verified the request."]},{"heading":"Protect confidentiality in the public filing","paragraphs":["Court fee applications can become public documents, so review every exhibit for client names, matter details, and privileged content. Redact what the local rule allows and keep an unredacted copy in a restricted folder for the court if it requests one. Do not assume that a sealed filing is automatic, because the procedure usually requires a motion and a supporting reason. Check the rule before the file leaves the office.","Apply the same discipline to metadata and to the file names that appear in the index. Remove comments, hidden columns, and prior versions that could reveal internal deliberation. Limit access to the working folder to the people who need it, and log who downloaded the final package. Confidentiality is part of the application's credibility, not a separate administrative task."]},{"heading":"Keep a reusable application template","paragraphs":["After the filing, save the checklist, the register structure, the exhibit index, and the review log as a template for the next appointment. Note the local rules that shaped the file and the questions the court actually asked. Remove client-specific content before the template is reused. A template built from a completed application is more reliable than a generic form.","Assign an owner to update the template when a rule or a judge's practice changes. Review it at the start of each new appointment rather than at the deadline. Keep a short changelog so the firm can see which version was used for which filing. The next application should start with the lessons from the last one, not from a blank folder."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who owns the fee application file?","a":"The responsible attorney owns the legal representations and the signature, while the billing assistant prepares the register, exhibits, and index for review. Record the owner in the application checklist so the signature path is visible. The same owner should confirm the final exhibit index before filing."},{"q":"What proves an entry is supportable?","a":"A reviewer can match the entry to a contemporaneous note or work product, the authorized rate, and the appointment order without asking the preparer to reconstruct the day. Keep the matching source record beside the register entry so the review is repeatable. A line that cannot be matched should appear in the exclusion log with the reason."},{"q":"When should the file be reviewed?","a":"Review the register and exhibits at least one week before the filing deadline, then again after any late correction or new expense receipt. Put the review date in the checklist and repeat it after any late correction. The version that was actually filed should be the one kept with the final record."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"set-a-flat-fee-matter-reconciliation-routine-before-the-next-invoice","title":"Set a flat-fee matter reconciliation routine before the next invoice","description":"A reconciliation routine that compares actual effort with the flat-fee assumption, separates scope change from estimate error, and records the decision.","category":"Flat fees","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/set-a-flat-fee-matter-reconciliation-routine-before-the-next-invoice.webp","internalService":"/services/prebill-preparation","internalSibling":"/blog/build-a-client-billing-guideline-change-briefing-for-timekeepers","source":{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},"sections":[{"heading":"Define what the flat fee includes","paragraphs":["A flat fee is only manageable when the engagement letter states what the fee covers and what it does not. List the included deliverables, the expected number of drafts, the meetings, and the travel that the fee assumes. Name the assumptions about client responsiveness and document volume that the estimate depends on. Without that baseline, every overrun looks like a performance problem instead of a scope question.","Translate the engagement letter into a one-page scope card that the billing team can read beside the time records. Include the fee amount, the payment schedule, the included tasks, and the written change process. Keep the client's approval of any assumption in the file. The card becomes the reference point for every later reconciliation conversation."]},{"heading":"Compare actual effort with the fee assumption","paragraphs":["At the halfway point, total the hours and costs recorded against the matter and compare them with the effort the fee assumed. Use the same task categories as the scope card so the comparison is meaningful. Note where the actual work is heavier and whether the difference is a one-time event or a pattern. A simple table is enough if the categories are honest.","Convert the hours to a notional value at the matter's standard rate only to understand the economics, not to bill the client. Keep that calculation internal and label it clearly. The purpose is to see whether the fee still covers the work and whether the firm needs to change the staffing or the scope. A notional value presented as a client charge would confuse the engagement."]},{"heading":"Separate scope change from estimate error","paragraphs":["A scope change is work the engagement letter did not include or a client decision that added effort. An estimate error is a miss in the original assumptions about volume, complexity, or turnaround. Code each variance as one or the other before discussing money with the client. The distinction determines whether the firm requests a supplement or absorbs the difference.","Write a short reason for each code and attach the evidence, such as a new document request, an added hearing, or a change in the client's review process. Review the codes with the responsible attorney rather than deciding alone. If the same estimate error appears in several matters, the problem is in the quoting process, not in one engagement. Keep the coded evidence with the matter file so a later reviewer can see how the distinction was made."]},{"heading":"Log out-of-scope work as it happens","paragraphs":["Ask the timekeepers to flag out-of-scope work when they record it, using a short note and a task code. The flag should describe the request and the person who made it, not just the extra hours. Route the flag to the billing owner within the same week so the client can be told before the work grows. A flag added at invoice time is a memory exercise, not a control.","Keep a running supplement log with the date, the request, the estimated effort, and the client's response. If the client declines the supplement, record that decision and the instruction to stop or continue. The log protects the firm if the client later disputes the fee. It also gives the next quote a realistic view of what clients actually ask for."]},{"heading":"Review the matter at the halfway point","paragraphs":["Schedule a short review when the matter reaches roughly half of the assumed effort or half of the expected duration. Invite the responsible attorney and the billing owner, and bring the scope card, the effort comparison, and the supplement log. Ask three questions: what changed, what remains, and who decides. End the meeting with one action and a date.","Do not wait for the prebill to discover that the fee is exhausted. A halfway review leaves room to adjust staffing, reset client expectations, or request a supplement while the work is still in progress. Record the decision in the matter file so the next reviewer understands why the plan changed. The meeting should take minutes, not an afternoon."]},{"heading":"Decide whether to renegotiate or absorb","paragraphs":["If the variance comes from a documented scope change, prepare a short supplement request that states the added work and the proposed fee. Send it before the additional work is completed when the client's approval is required. If the variance is an estimate error, decide whether the firm will absorb it as a quoting lesson or discuss a revised fee openly. Either choice needs a written record.","Involve the responsible attorney in the client conversation and keep the billing team in a preparation role. Do not let an assistant negotiate the fee or promise a credit without authority. Give the client one clear option and the reason behind it. A calm, early conversation is easier than a dispute at collection time."]},{"heading":"Document the decision for finance","paragraphs":["Record the final fee treatment in the billing system with a note that explains the supplement, the absorption, or the credit. Attach the client's approval and the scope card version that applied. Make sure the invoice reflects the decision and that no time is billed twice. Finance should be able to reconcile the invoice to the engagement terms without calling the attorney.","Update the matter's profitability view with the actual effort and the final fee. Separate the fee outcome from the write-off or adjustment so the reporting stays clear. If the firm uses a realization metric, note how the flat fee affects it. Consistent coding keeps the next flat-fee analysis comparable."]},{"heading":"Carry the lesson into the next fee quote","paragraphs":["At the end of the matter, compare the original assumptions with the actual effort and record the largest drivers. Add those drivers to the quoting checklist, such as the number of client review cycles or the volume of third-party documents. Share the lesson with the people who quote similar work. A quote improves faster when the misses are named.","Review the flat-fee portfolio each quarter to see whether the same task category is consistently underestimated. Adjust the template or the staffing model rather than blaming individual timekeepers. Keep the client-facing fee simple while the internal analysis stays detailed. The goal is a fee the client understands and a scope the firm can deliver."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who decides whether to request a supplement?","a":"The responsible attorney decides after reviewing the scope card and the supplement log, while the billing assistant prepares the comparison and the draft request. Keep the decision note with the scope card so the fee history is easy to reconstruct. The note should state the amount and the date of the client conversation."},{"q":"What evidence supports a scope change?","a":"A dated client request, a new document or hearing, or a written change in the review process that falls outside the engagement letter's included tasks. Attach the client request to the supplement log and reference it in the fee note. A verbal change should be confirmed in writing before the extra work continues."},{"q":"How often should the matter be reviewed?","a":"Review at the halfway point, at any major scope change, and again before the prebill so the fee decision is made while work can still be adjusted. Review at the halfway point, after any scope change, and again before the prebill. Waiting until the invoice is issued removes most of the available options."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"build-a-client-billing-guideline-change-briefing-for-timekeepers","title":"Build a client billing guideline change briefing for timekeepers","description":"A briefing routine that turns a guideline change into examples, effective dates, owner contacts, and a first-cycle audit sample timekeepers can follow.","category":"Billing guidelines","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/build-a-client-billing-guideline-change-briefing-for-timekeepers.webp","internalService":"/services/time-entry-review-support","internalSibling":"/blog/design-a-billing-role-scorecard-that-measures-evidence-quality","source":{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},"sections":[{"heading":"Capture the change and its source","paragraphs":["A guideline change can arrive in an updated outside counsel policy, a portal message, an email from the client's billing contact, or a revised engagement letter. Save the source document, the date it was received, and the name of the person who sent it. Highlight the specific clauses that changed instead of summarizing the whole policy again. The briefing should begin with the exact language the timekeeper must follow.","Ask the client contact to confirm the effective date and whether the change applies to open matters, new matters, or both. If the answer is unclear, write the question down and send it before drafting the briefing. Do not assume that a policy posted this month applies to work performed last month. The effective date controls which entries the reviewer will test."]},{"heading":"Identify which matters and timekeepers it affects","paragraphs":["List the clients and matters that use the changed guideline and the timekeepers who bill them. Include anyone who reviews or approves time for those matters, because a reviewer who does not know the change will approve the old pattern. Check the matter record for the correct guideline version and for any client-specific exception. The briefing audience is the people who can change an entry before it reaches the prebill.","If the change affects only one task code or one expense category, say so and avoid a general training session. A targeted note to the affected group is easier to read and easier to confirm. Keep a distribution list that can be reused for the next update. The list should name people, not just a team mailbox."]},{"heading":"Translate the rule into daily entry behavior","paragraphs":["Write each change as an instruction the timekeeper can apply while entering time. Replace abstract language such as be specific with a concrete pattern that shows the task, the document, and the result. State what to do when the information is not yet available, such as using a placeholder and returning to complete the entry. A rule that cannot be applied at the keyboard will not survive a busy week.","Group the instructions by the moment they matter: before the entry, during the narrative, and at the prebill review. Add a short note about the evidence the reviewer will check. If the client requires a task code, a phase code, or a fixed narrative format, show exactly where it goes. The goal is a briefing that a timekeeper can keep open beside the entry screen."]},{"heading":"Show before-and-after examples","paragraphs":["Use two or three examples from the firm's own redacted entries to show the old pattern and the corrected pattern. Choose examples that are close to the work the audience actually does, not generic placeholders. Mark the reason for the correction so the reader learns the rule, not just the wording. Remove client names and amounts before circulating the examples.","Ask a timekeeper from the affected group to review the examples for realism. If the corrected version looks unnatural, the briefing will produce stiff entries that still fail review. Revise the example until it sounds like a clear record of real work. Keep the final set short enough to read in one sitting."]},{"heading":"Set the effective date and the first review","paragraphs":["Put the effective date at the top of the briefing and state which entries will be reviewed under the new rule. Schedule a first-cycle review of a small sample, such as the first ten entries from each affected timekeeper. Tell the timekeepers that the sample is a learning check, not a punishment. The review should catch misunderstandings while they are cheap to fix.","Decide who will review the sample and how quickly the feedback will return. Give the reviewer a short checklist based on the changed clauses. Record the result and the questions that came up during the first cycle. If the sample shows a pattern of confusion, revise the briefing rather than repeating it unchanged."]},{"heading":"Route questions to a named owner","paragraphs":["Name one person who will answer guideline questions and one backup for that person. Give the timekeepers a clear channel and a response expectation, such as one business day. Keep a question log with the date, the question, and the answer so the same issue is not resolved differently by different reviewers. A named owner prevents the client contact from being copied on every routine question.","When the answer changes the briefing, update the document and tell the affected group. Do not leave the correction buried in an email thread. If the client must decide, route the question through the billing owner rather than letting a timekeeper negotiate the rule. The escalation path should be visible in the briefing itself."]},{"heading":"Confirm the briefing reached each timekeeper","paragraphs":["Track delivery separately from reading. A message that was sent is not evidence that the timekeeper understood the change. Use a short acknowledgment question or a quick quiz that asks the timekeeper to apply the rule to one example. Follow up with anyone who has not responded before the effective date. Keep the confirmation record with the briefing version.","For a large group, ask the practice group leaders to confirm their teams rather than chasing every person centrally. Report the completion rate to the billing owner without naming individuals in a public summary. The point is coverage, not surveillance. A briefing that reaches only half the group will produce a mixed sample and a confusing review."]},{"heading":"Update the guideline library and the audit sample","paragraphs":["Replace the old guideline version in the library and mark the change date so reviewers can tell which rule applied to which period. Add the changed clause to the standard audit sample for the next two cycles. If the client later reverses the change, keep the prior version and record the reversal. A library that only holds the current version cannot explain a historical entry.","At the next quarterly review, compare the audit results with the briefing's examples and questions. If the same defect keeps appearing, the problem may be the briefing, the system, or the client's instruction rather than the timekeeper. Update the training material and the review checklist together. The briefing is part of a loop, not a one-time announcement."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who confirms that a guideline change applies to open matters?","a":"The billing owner asks the client contact and records the answer in the matter file before the briefing is circulated. The billing owner should confirm the answer with the client and update the matter file. An oral confirmation should be followed by a short written note."},{"q":"What makes a guideline briefing effective?","a":"It shows the exact changed clause, the effective date, a realistic before-and-after example, and the person to ask when the rule is unclear. The briefing should show the changed clause, the effective date, a realistic example, and the named contact. A short applied question is more useful than a long policy summary."},{"q":"How is completion measured?","a":"Delivery is tracked separately from understanding, using a short applied question and a follow-up for anyone who has not responded before the effective date. Track delivery separately from understanding and follow up before the effective date. The confirmation record should be stored with the briefing version."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"design-a-billing-role-scorecard-that-measures-evidence-quality","title":"Design a billing role scorecard that measures evidence quality","description":"A scorecard that scores completeness, rework, and exception handling instead of raw volume, with calibration and a privacy-safe review rhythm.","category":"Role design","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/design-a-billing-role-scorecard-that-measures-evidence-quality.webp","internalService":"/services/billing-reporting-support","internalSibling":"/blog/write-a-matter-closure-billing-checklist-for-departing-timekeepers","source":{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},"sections":[{"heading":"Start with the output the role owns","paragraphs":["A billing support role produces review-ready records, not activity. Start the scorecard with the outputs the role is accountable for, such as a complete prebill packet, a reconciled cost log, or a rejection queue with named owners. Describe each output in a way a reviewer can check without watching the person work. If the output cannot be defined, the role boundary is still unclear.","Separate the outputs the role prepares from the decisions an attorney or billing manager owns. The scorecard should measure the quality of the preparation and the timeliness of the escalation, not the outcome of a judgment call. A good scorecard makes that distinction visible. A scorecard that rewards a favorable write-off decision will push the assistant toward work the role should not do."]},{"heading":"Measure completeness before volume","paragraphs":["Count the records that contain every required field before counting how many records were touched. Volume without completeness creates rework downstream and hides the real cost of the role. Use a short field list tied to the workflow, such as source, owner, date, amount, and next action. Report the complete share beside the total so a high volume cannot mask a low quality rate.","Define what partial and missing mean before the first scoring period. A record with a source but no owner is partial, while a record with no source at all is missing. Keep the definitions in the scorecard guide so two reviewers apply them the same way. The measure should point to a specific repair, not just a percentage."]},{"heading":"Score the evidence, not the activity","paragraphs":["A scorecard can count logins, messages, or entries, but those numbers say little about whether the work can be reviewed. Replace activity counts with evidence checks, such as whether the packet includes the client authorization or whether the rejection note explains the correction. Ask the reviewer to sample the evidence rather than rely on a self-report. The sample can be small if the definition is clear.","Use a simple scale, such as complete, partial, or missing, and keep it consistent across periods. Avoid a weighted score that hides a serious gap behind several minor successes. If a record lacks the one document that proves authority, the score should reflect that failure plainly. The role holder needs to know which gap matters most."]},{"heading":"Add a rework and exception measure","paragraphs":["Measure how often a prepared record comes back for correction and what caused the return. Group the causes into missing evidence, wrong matter, unclear instruction, system issue, and judgment question. A high rework rate may point to the handoff, the template, or the training rather than to the person. Report the cause distribution with the rate so the team can choose a fix.","Track exceptions that were escalated correctly as a positive sign, not a failure. A role that never escalates may be guessing, while a role that escalates everything may lack decision rights. Review a sample of escalations to see whether the evidence and the question were clear. The scorecard should reward an early, well-documented escalation."]},{"heading":"Keep client and matter privacy in the scorecard","paragraphs":["Score at the level of the queue, the task type, or the role, not the client name. Remove amounts, matter descriptions, and personal details that are not needed for the review. If an example is used in coaching, redact it and use a role label. The scorecard is a management tool, not a second client record.","Limit access to the underlying sample and keep it in a controlled folder. Aggregate before sharing results beyond the manager and the role holder. Check that a small group cannot be re-identified from a chart or a table. Privacy rules apply to internal performance data just as they apply to client files."]},{"heading":"Calibrate with a second reviewer","paragraphs":["Have a second reviewer score the same sample before the scorecard is used for a formal review. Compare the results at the record level and discuss every disagreement. If the reviewers interpret a field differently, revise the definition rather than averaging the scores. Calibration is especially important in the first two periods.","Record the agreement rate and the changes made to the guide. Repeat the calibration after a template or system change, because the old definitions may no longer fit. A scorecard that drifts between reviewers is not a fair measure. The calibration note should be part of the scorecard's own evidence."]},{"heading":"Review the scorecard with the role holder","paragraphs":["Give the role holder the sample, the definitions, and the result before the review meeting. Ask which gaps are caused by missing access, unclear instructions, or an unrealistic deadline. Listen for the workarounds that the scorecard does not capture. The conversation should produce one process change and one support change.","Separate coaching from evaluation and be explicit about which is happening. Use the scorecard to improve the workflow, not to rank people against each other. If a measure consistently punishes a role for a system problem, remove or redesign it. A scorecard that the role holder cannot influence will be ignored."]},{"heading":"Retire measures that reward the wrong work","paragraphs":["Review the scorecard each quarter and remove any measure that encourages speed over evidence. Watch for gaming, such as closing records without the required source or escalating routine work to avoid a quality check. Compare the scorecard with the actual downstream rework and client questions. A measure that looks good while rework rises is measuring the wrong thing.","Keep a short history of the measures and the reasons they changed. Tell the role holder what changed and why so the revision does not feel arbitrary. Add new measures only when the team can define them and review them consistently. The best scorecard is small, stable, and tied to the output the role actually owns."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who owns the scorecard?","a":"The billing manager owns the scorecard and the review conversation, while the role holder helps define the evidence and the constraints that affect the result. The billing manager owns the review and the role holder helps define the evidence. Both should agree on the constraints before the first scoring period."},{"q":"What should a scorecard measure first?","a":"Start with the completeness of the output and the rate of rework, then add exception handling and timeliness once the definitions are stable. Begin with completeness and rework, then add exception handling once the definitions are stable. A small, stable set of measures is easier to calibrate."},{"q":"How is fairness protected?","a":"A second reviewer calibrates the sample, definitions are written down, and aggregate results are reviewed with the role holder before any formal evaluation. A second reviewer checks the sample and the definitions are written down. Aggregate results are discussed with the role holder before any formal evaluation."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"write-a-matter-closure-billing-checklist-for-departing-timekeepers","title":"Write a matter closure billing checklist for departing timekeepers","description":"A closure checklist that captures open time, costs, narratives, billing ownership, and access before a timekeeper leaves the matter.","category":"Billing operations","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/write-a-matter-closure-billing-checklist-for-departing-timekeepers.webp","internalService":"/services/matter-record-upkeep","internalSibling":"/blog/build-a-vendor-cost-invoice-review-queue-for-the-billing-team","source":{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},"sections":[{"heading":"Trigger the checklist before the last day","paragraphs":["A departure is a billing risk long before the final paycheck. Start the checklist when the firm learns that a timekeeper will leave, not on the last afternoon. Identify the matters where the person is the primary timekeeper or the only person with context. Give the billing owner a named contact and a target date for each open item. Early notice turns a scramble into a planned handoff.","If the departure is sudden, use the same checklist but shorten the timeline and prioritize the matters with the nearest deadlines. Do not wait for a complete knowledge transfer before securing the open time and the client commitments. Record what is known and what is still missing. The checklist should work in both planned and unplanned situations."]},{"heading":"List the open time and cost items","paragraphs":["Pull the unbilled time, the unposted costs, and the draft entries that have not been released. Check for time recorded on the last few days that may still be in a personal draft or a calendar note. Ask the departing timekeeper to identify any work that was performed but not entered. A missing entry is harder to recover after the person has left.","Match each open cost to a receipt and a client authorization before the file moves. Flag costs that were incurred but not yet approved, and route them to the new billing owner. Do not close the matter with an unexplained balance in a cost ledger. The closure checklist should show zero unowned items, even when an item is still pending approval."]},{"heading":"Transfer narrative context, not just files","paragraphs":["A file folder without context can take hours to reconstruct. Ask the timekeeper to record a short note for each open matter that explains the work completed, the next step, and the reason for any unusual entry. Include the client's current expectation and any promised date. The note should be specific enough for a reviewer who has never worked the matter.","Schedule a short handoff conversation with the receiving timekeeper and the billing owner. Use the notes as the agenda and record any correction in the matter file. Do not rely on a verbal handoff alone, because the details fade quickly. The written note is the record that survives the conversation."]},{"heading":"Confirm the billing owner for each matter","paragraphs":["Name the person who will approve time, costs, and client communication after the departure. Update the matter record and the billing system so the old name no longer appears as the owner. Tell the client contact who is taking over and how to reach them. An unassigned matter will drift until an invoice or a deadline forces someone to notice.","Check the delegation and the approval limits attached to the new owner. A name in a field is not enough if the person lacks system access or authority for the amount. Test one approval with the new owner before the next invoice. The closure checklist should end with a working approval path, not just a reassignment."]},{"heading":"Close or reassign the remaining work in progress","paragraphs":["Decide which open tasks can be completed by the departing timekeeper before the last day and which must move. Give priority to work that is close to a billable milestone or a client deadline. Record the decision and the new owner in the matter file. Avoid leaving a task in a half-finished state with no note.","For each reassigned task, confirm that the new timekeeper has the source documents and the client's instructions. Transfer the relevant calendar entries and reminders as well as the files. If a task will not be completed, tell the client and record the reason. A clean reassignment protects the client relationship and the invoice."]},{"heading":"Check access and delegation after departure","paragraphs":["Disable the departing person's system access on the agreed date and confirm that shared inboxes and document folders still have an owner. Check for rules, forwarding, or saved searches that were tied to the person. Move any personal working files that belong to the firm into the matter folder. Access cleanup is part of billing continuity, not just IT security.","Review the delegation settings in the billing and e-billing systems so new work does not route to an inactive user. Test a submission or an approval from the new owner's account. Keep a record of the access change and the date. An invoice rejected because the approver no longer exists is an avoidable closure failure."]},{"heading":"Record the handoff in the matter file","paragraphs":["Add a short closure note that lists the open items, the decisions, the new owners, and the dates. Link the note to the handoff record and the client communication. The note should let a reviewer understand the state of the matter without reading a long email chain. Keep it factual and free of speculation about the departure.","If the matter itself is closing, complete the final invoice review and the trust reconciliation before the file is archived. Confirm that no time or cost remains unposted. Store the closure note with the final records according to the retention schedule. A matter that is closed in the system but open in a personal folder is not closed."]},{"heading":"Review the checklist after the first closure","paragraphs":["After the first matter closes under the checklist, ask the new owner what was missing and what took the longest. Update the checklist with the specific gap, such as a missing cost receipt or an unclear client contact. Remove steps that did not add value. The checklist should get shorter and sharper with use.","Share the lesson with the billing team and the practice group without naming the departing person. Add the common gaps to the onboarding material for the next timekeeper. Review the checklist whenever the billing system or the delegation rules change. The goal is a repeatable handoff that does not depend on who is leaving."]}],"faq":[{"q":"When should the closure checklist start?","a":"Start as soon as the firm knows about the departure, and prioritize matters with the nearest client or invoice deadlines if the notice is short. Start when the departure is known and prioritize matters with the nearest deadlines. A sudden departure uses the same checklist on a shorter timeline."},{"q":"Who owns the matter after the timekeeper leaves?","a":"A named receiving timekeeper owns the legal work and a billing owner approves time and costs, with both recorded in the matter file before the last day. A receiving timekeeper owns the legal work and a billing owner approves time and costs. Both names should be in the matter record before the last day."},{"q":"What is the most commonly missed item?","a":"Unposted time and costs are the most common gap, so the checklist pulls drafts, calendar notes, and unreleased entries before access is removed. Unposted time and costs are the most common gap. Check drafts, calendar notes, and unreleased entries before access is removed."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"build-a-vendor-cost-invoice-review-queue-for-the-billing-team","title":"Build a vendor cost invoice review queue for the billing team","description":"A vendor invoice queue that matches each cost to a matter, authorization, and receipt before the charge reaches a client invoice.","category":"Disbursements","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/build-a-vendor-cost-invoice-review-queue-for-the-billing-team.webp","internalService":"/services/invoice-follow-up","internalSibling":"/blog/prepare-a-billing-audit-sample-request-without-disrupting-the-close","source":{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},"sections":[{"heading":"Separate vendor invoices from client invoices","paragraphs":["A vendor invoice is a request for payment from a supplier, while a client invoice is a request for reimbursement or fees from the client. Mixing the two queues makes it hard to see which costs are still unapproved and which have already been billed. Give the vendor queue its own owner, status values, and review deadline. The separation also prevents a vendor charge from being paid twice through two different paths.","List the vendor types the firm actually uses, such as court reporters, process servers, experts, couriers, and copy services. Note which vendors bill per matter and which bill a monthly summary. A monthly summary needs an allocation step before it can enter the client queue. The queue design should reflect how the invoices actually arrive."]},{"heading":"Capture the matter and client authorization","paragraphs":["Every vendor invoice needs a matter reference and a client authorization before it can be treated as a billable cost. The authorization may be in the engagement letter, a budget approval, or a written client instruction for a specific expense. If the authorization is unclear, route the invoice to the billing owner with the question. Do not assume that a cost is billable because the firm incurred it.","Record the authorization source and the date in the queue row. For a recurring vendor, keep the approved scope and the rate on file so routine invoices do not require a new approval each month. Recheck the approval when the amount or the scope changes. A clear authorization trail is the difference between a recoverable cost and a write-off."]},{"heading":"Match the vendor line to the cost ledger","paragraphs":["Compare the vendor invoice number, date, amount, and matter with the cost ledger before approval. Check that the invoice has not already been posted under a different reference. Look for a credit, a partial payment, or a prior adjustment that changes the amount due. The queue should show the ledger entry beside the invoice so the reviewer can see the match.","If the vendor charges several matters on one invoice, split the allocation and record the basis for each share. Keep the original invoice intact and link the allocation to it. Do not post a rounded share without a note, because the client may ask for the calculation. A clean allocation is easier to defend than a single blended line."]},{"heading":"Flag duplicate, split, and unusual lines","paragraphs":["Duplicates often appear when a vendor resends an invoice or when two staff members upload the same document. Compare the invoice number, the date, and the amount across open and paid records. Watch for split invoices that fall just below an approval threshold, because they may be an attempt to avoid review. Flag unusual descriptions, rates, or quantities for a second look.","Give the reviewer a short reason code for each flag, such as duplicate, split, rate variance, missing receipt, or unclear matter. Resolve the flag before the cost reaches the client invoice. If the vendor made an error, request a corrected invoice in writing. A flagged item that is quietly paid teaches the vendor that the control is optional."]},{"heading":"Route approvals by amount and cost type","paragraphs":["Set approval limits that match the firm's authority rules, and route each invoice to the right owner. A routine courier charge may need only the billing owner, while an expert fee may need the responsible attorney and the client. Put the limit and the approver on the queue row so the path is visible. Do not let an urgent invoice bypass the limit without a written exception.","Track the approval date and the approver for each cost. If an approver is unavailable, name a backup rather than leaving the invoice in a personal inbox. The queue should show how long each item has waited and which items are close to a payment deadline. Approval delay is a cost risk even when the invoice itself is correct."]},{"heading":"Handle tax and currency questions explicitly","paragraphs":["Record whether the vendor charge includes tax, whether the firm can recover it, and how the client invoice should show it. For a foreign vendor, capture the currency, the exchange rate, and the date of conversion. Do not silently absorb a difference or round it into another line. The client should be able to see the basis for the amount.","Ask the finance or tax owner when the treatment is unclear, and keep the answer with the invoice. If a client disputes a tax charge, the firm needs the vendor documentation and the internal decision. Apply the same treatment consistently across similar vendors. An inconsistent tax line is a common source of client questions."]},{"heading":"Track the review deadline and the accrual","paragraphs":["Each vendor invoice has a payment deadline and an accounting period, and the queue should show both. Post the cost to the correct period even when the client invoice will be issued later. Mark invoices that are approved but not yet paid so the accrual stays accurate. A missed accrual distorts the matter's profitability and the month-end report.","Review the queue at least weekly and before every client invoice run. Clear the items that are ready, escalate the ones that are stuck, and note the reason for any hold. Reconcile the queue total with the cost ledger at month end. The reconciliation is the proof that no approved cost is sitting outside the accounting system."]},{"heading":"Reconcile paid vendor invoices each month","paragraphs":["After payment, match the paid amount to the approved invoice, the ledger entry, and the client billing status. Investigate any difference, including a short payment, a credit, or a duplicate. Update the queue status so the item does not remain open after payment. A paid invoice that still looks open will distort the next review.","Keep the vendor statement and the payment confirmation with the invoice record. If the vendor statement shows an invoice the firm does not recognize, resolve it before the next payment run. Report the number of open items and the value of unapproved costs to the billing owner each month. The queue is a control, not just a filing cabinet."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who approves a vendor cost?","a":"The approval follows the firm's authority limits and the client's authorization, with the billing owner preparing the match and the responsible attorney approving larger or client-specific costs. Approval follows the firm limits and the client authorization. The billing owner prepares the match and the attorney approves larger costs."},{"q":"What belongs in the vendor queue?","a":"Every vendor invoice that may become a client cost, including its matter reference, authorization, ledger match, approval status, and payment date. Every vendor invoice that may become a client cost belongs in the queue. The row should show the matter, authorization, ledger match, approval, and payment date."},{"q":"How are duplicates prevented?","a":"The queue checks invoice number, date, amount, and ledger reference against both open and paid records before a cost is approved or billed. Check invoice number, date, amount, and ledger reference against open and paid records. A second reviewer resolves any match that is not exact."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"prepare-a-billing-audit-sample-request-without-disrupting-the-close","title":"Prepare a billing audit sample request without disrupting the close","description":"A response routine for billing audit sample requests that defines the population, secures the evidence, and protects the monthly close calendar.","category":"Audit support","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/prepare-a-billing-audit-sample-request-without-disrupting-the-close.webp","internalService":"/services/billing-reporting-support","internalSibling":"/blog/build-a-partner-billing-delegation-agreement-that-names-decision-rights","source":{"label":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},"sections":[{"heading":"Read the request before pulling records","paragraphs":["An audit sample request usually names a period, a population, a sample size, and the fields the auditor wants to see. Read the whole request before exporting anything, because a narrow reading can produce an incomplete response and a second request. Clarify the unit of the sample, such as an invoice, a time entry, or a cost line. Write the answer down and confirm it with the auditor.","Identify the business owner and the single point of contact for the response. Copying the whole billing team on every message creates conflicting answers and duplicate work. Give the auditor one channel and one expected response time. A calm, clear process reduces the disruption to the close."]},{"heading":"Agree the sample definition and period","paragraphs":["Confirm how the population is bounded, including credits, write-offs, voided invoices, and invoices that are still open. Ask whether the auditor wants the sample drawn by the firm or provided by the auditor. If the firm draws it, document the method and keep the random seed or selection list. A sample that cannot be reproduced will be questioned later.","Match the period to the firm's accounting calendar and note any cutoff difference between the billing system and the general ledger. Record the query used and the date it was run so the population can be recreated. If the population changes after the sample is drawn, tell the auditor rather than quietly replacing records. Transparency about the method is part of the evidence."]},{"heading":"Build a secure evidence folder","paragraphs":["Create one controlled folder with subfolders for the request, the population, the sample, the evidence, and the response. Name the files with the sample number so the auditor can follow the index. Remove unrelated client material before sharing the folder. Limit access to the people who are preparing or reviewing the response.","Keep a manifest that lists each sample item, the documents provided, and the person who supplied them. Use a consistent file format and avoid renaming files after they are sent. If a document is too large, provide a link through an approved secure channel rather than an open file share. The evidence folder should be easy to audit on its own."]},{"heading":"Redact what the auditor does not need","paragraphs":["Share the records that answer the request, not the entire matter file. Redact unrelated client names, privileged communications, and personal data that the audit does not require. Check the firm's confidentiality obligations and the client's audit rights before sending. If the auditor asks for more, route the request through the billing owner and document the reason.","Apply redaction to metadata as well as visible text, including document properties and hidden columns. Keep an unredacted copy in the controlled folder for internal reference. Do not alter the underlying transaction record, because an auditor may compare it with the system. Redaction should remove exposure without changing the evidence."]},{"heading":"Answer with source records, not summaries","paragraphs":["When the auditor asks for support, provide the source document that proves the entry rather than a freshly written summary. A time entry should link to a contemporaneous note or work product, and a cost should link to the vendor invoice and the approval. Add a short cover note that explains the sample number and the document set. The note should not replace the evidence.","If a source record is missing, say so plainly and explain what was done to locate it. Do not create a replacement document that looks contemporaneous. An honest gap with a remediation note is more credible than a reconstructed record. The auditor is testing the control, not just the individual item."]},{"heading":"Keep a question log and a single owner","paragraphs":["Log every question, the date, the answer, and the person who answered it. Reuse the answers for similar sample items so the response stays consistent. If a question changes the scope, update the request summary and tell the auditor. The log protects the firm if a later reviewer asks why a document was or was not provided.","Keep the owner responsible for the response until the audit closes, even if the fieldwork moves to another team member. Brief the backup on the open items and the agreed definitions. A handoff without the log will produce inconsistent answers. The owner should also track the close tasks that were displaced by the audit."]},{"heading":"Protect the close calendar while responding","paragraphs":["Block time for the audit response and identify the close tasks that cannot move. Delegate the routine close preparation and keep the judgment calls with the billing owner. Set a daily limit on audit interruptions so the response does not consume the whole team. Tell the auditor when the next batch will arrive instead of answering one message at a time.","If the request is larger than expected, ask for a phased delivery and agree the order of the sample items. Prioritize the items that affect the close or a client deadline. Record the tradeoff in the close log so the delay is visible. A controlled response is better than a rushed one that creates rework."]},{"heading":"Close the audit with a lessons note","paragraphs":["When the fieldwork ends, record the findings, the questions that recurred, and the documents that were hard to find. Separate a control gap from a documentation gap, because the fixes are different. Share the note with the billing team and the process owner. The next audit should not repeat the same search.","Update the sample response template and the evidence folder structure with what worked. Add any missing source requirement to the billing checklist. Keep the audit file according to the retention schedule and remove access for people who no longer need it. A closed audit is an opportunity to make the next one smaller."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who should own an audit sample response?","a":"One billing owner owns the response and the question log, with the finance or compliance team consulted on scope and confidentiality. One billing owner owns the response and the question log. Finance or compliance is consulted on scope and confidentiality."},{"q":"What should be shared with the auditor?","a":"Only the records that answer the agreed sample request, with unrelated client and privileged material redacted and the source evidence left intact. Share only the records that answer the agreed request. Redact unrelated client and privileged material while leaving the source evidence intact."},{"q":"How is the close protected?","a":"The team phases the response, sets a daily interruption limit, and records any close task that moves so the tradeoff stays visible. Phase the response and set a daily interruption limit. Record any close task that moves so the tradeoff stays visible."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"},{"slug":"build-a-partner-billing-delegation-agreement-that-names-decision-rights","title":"Build a partner billing delegation agreement that names decision rights","description":"A written delegation agreement that defines preparation, review, approval, escalation triggers, and the evidence standard for a billing support role.","category":"Role design","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/build-a-partner-billing-delegation-agreement-that-names-decision-rights.webp","internalService":"/services/prebill-preparation","internalSibling":"/blog/prepare-a-court-appointed-fee-application-support-file","source":{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},"sections":[{"heading":"Start with the decisions the partner owns","paragraphs":["A delegation agreement works best when it starts with the decisions that stay with the partner. Fee adjustments, client commitments, confidentiality calls, and legal strategy belong to the attorney, not to an assistant. Write those decisions down before describing the tasks that can move. The agreement should make the boundary obvious to both people on a busy day.","Distinguish a decision from a preparation task. Preparing a write-off packet is not the same as approving the write-off, and drafting a client note is not the same as sending it. Use plain verbs such as prepare, check, recommend, approve, and send. The verb list becomes the shared vocabulary for the role."]},{"heading":"Define preparation, review, and approval","paragraphs":["Describe what the assistant prepares, what the partner reviews, and what the partner approves. For each output, name the source records, the required fields, and the point at which the work is ready for review. A prebill packet, for example, may be prepared with a reconciliation and an exception list, then reviewed by the partner before release. The sequence should be written, not assumed.","Set a service expectation for each step so the partner knows when to expect the review. A short turnaround for a routine prebill and a longer window for a complex matter are both reasonable if they are stated. Record what happens when the partner is unavailable. A delegation agreement without a backup path will stall at the first trip."]},{"heading":"Write the escalation triggers","paragraphs":["List the conditions that require the assistant to stop and escalate rather than decide. Common triggers include a missing client authorization, a fee change above a set amount, a conflict between the engagement letter and the client's instruction, and any request to communicate a legal position. Keep the list short enough to remember and specific enough to apply. Review the triggers after the first month and add any situation that actually required a judgment the assistant could not make.","For each trigger, name the person to contact and the information to include. The assistant should be able to escalate with the source, the question, and the deadline in one message. A vague escalation creates another round of questions. Review the triggers after the first month and add the situations that actually occurred."]},{"heading":"Set the evidence standard for each task","paragraphs":["The agreement should say what a complete record looks like for each delegated task. A time entry needs a narrative that identifies the task, the document, and the result. A cost needs a receipt, a matter reference, and an approval. A rejection needs the portal code, the line, and the correction. The standard should be checkable by someone who did not do the work.","Keep the standards in a short appendix rather than scattering them through the agreement. Link each standard to the template or system field that supports it. If a standard cannot be met because the system lacks a field, fix the template or record the exception. An evidence standard that the tool cannot capture will be ignored."]},{"heading":"Agree the communication boundary","paragraphs":["Decide which messages the assistant may send, which must be drafted for the partner, and which must come from the partner. Client fee negotiations, legal advice, and sensitive relationship issues usually stay with the attorney. Routine status updates and document requests may be delegated with a clear template. Write the boundary by audience and topic, not just by channel.","Give the assistant approved language for the common messages so the tone stays consistent. Include a rule for copying the partner on any message that touches a disputed amount or a deadline. Review a sample of sent messages in the first month. The communication boundary protects the client relationship and the firm's professional obligations."]},{"heading":"Name the backup and the review cadence","paragraphs":["A delegation agreement needs a named backup for both the assistant and the partner. The backup should have the access, the context, and the authority to keep the queue moving. Test the backup once during a planned absence. An untested backup is a name on a document, not a control.","Set a short weekly check-in and a longer monthly review. The weekly check-in covers the open queue, the exceptions, and the next deadlines. The monthly review looks at the evidence sample, the rework, and any change to the client's requirements. Keep the notes brief and store them where both people can find them."]},{"heading":"Test the agreement on one live matter","paragraphs":["Choose a matter with a clear scope and a partner who will give honest feedback. Run the agreement for one billing cycle and record where the role had to ask for a decision that was not covered. Note the tasks that took longer than expected and the reviews that were skipped. The test should produce a short list of changes, not a pass or fail grade.","Revise the agreement with the partner and the assistant together. Add the missing triggers, remove the steps that added no value, and clarify the evidence standard that caused the most questions. Keep the old version so the change is visible. A tested agreement is more credible than a template that was never used."]},{"heading":"Revise it when the role or client changes","paragraphs":["Review the agreement when the assistant changes, when the partner changes, or when a client adopts new billing guidelines. A new client requirement can move a task from routine preparation into a decision that needs approval. Update the evidence standard and the escalation list at the same time. A stale agreement is a source of quiet errors.","Keep the current version in the shared workspace and remove old copies from personal folders. Note the effective date and the person who approved the change. Give the assistant a chance to raise the parts that no longer work in practice. The agreement should evolve with the work, not sit in a drawer after the first review."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who owns the delegated decisions?","a":"The partner keeps fee, client, confidentiality, and legal decisions, while the assistant prepares evidence, checks completeness, and escalates anything outside the written rule. The partner keeps fee, client, confidentiality, and legal decisions. The assistant prepares evidence, checks completeness, and escalates outside the written rule."},{"q":"What makes a delegation agreement usable?","a":"It names the preparation, review, and approval steps, the escalation triggers, the evidence standard, and the backup in plain language. Name the preparation, review, and approval steps in plain language. Include the escalation triggers, the evidence standard, and the backup."},{"q":"How often should it be reviewed?","a":"Review it weekly during the first month, then monthly and whenever the role, the partner, or the client's billing requirements change. Review weekly during the first month, then monthly. Repeat the review whenever the role, partner, or client requirements change."}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10"}];

export const november10ResearchPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"how-much-does-scope-change-explain-flat-fee-matter-variance","title":"How much does scope change explain flat-fee matter variance?","summary":"A variance study that separates client-driven scope change from estimating error in flat-fee matters and reports the share each explains.","cluster":"Budgets","finding":"Flat-fee variance is often treated as a delivery failure, but a large share usually traces to documented scope change that was never priced into the original fee.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks how much of the effort variance in flat-fee matters is explained by documented scope change. The unit is a closed flat-fee matter, and the measure is the difference between the effort assumed in the fee quote and the effort actually recorded. The study separates client-driven additions from errors in the firm's original estimate. It does not attempt to judge whether the fee was fair or whether the timekeeper worked efficiently.","Scope the study to matters that closed during the period with a written flat-fee quote and a recorded effort total. Include matters that ended early, matters that were extended, and matters that produced a credit or supplement. Exclude matters with no written scope card, because the baseline cannot be reconstructed reliably. State the practice group, the systems used, and the owner of the analysis."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a scope change as work the written quote did not include, requested or caused by the client after the quote was accepted. Examples include a new document production, an added hearing, a second review cycle, or a change in the client's approval process. A change in internal staffing is not a scope change for this study. Write two examples and one boundary case so reviewers apply the same rule.","Define an estimate error as a variance that comes from the firm's assumptions about volume, complexity, or turnaround at the time of the quote. If the client asked for exactly what the quote described and the work still took longer, the difference is an estimate error. Keep the two categories separate even when a matter has both. A blended category would hide the repair that each one needs."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every closed flat-fee matter with a written quote and a recorded effort total in the period. Report the number of matters, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. Count matters rather than invoices, because one matter may produce several invoices. If a matter is split across offices or systems, state how the effort was combined.","Use the quoted effort, not the fee amount, as the denominator for the variance measure. Convert the fee to a notional effort only when the quote recorded an assumed hour total, and label that conversion clearly. A fee without an assumed effort can still be studied for scope events, but it cannot support an hours variance. Report the two analyses separately if both are needed."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the signed engagement letter, the scope card, the time records, and the supplement log as the primary evidence. Compare the quoted assumptions with the recorded effort and with the client's written requests. Record the date each source was observed and the system it came from. If the scope card is missing, mark the matter as incomplete rather than inferring the original scope from memory.","Rank the evidence so reviewers know what to trust first. A signed quote and a dated client request are stronger than a later summary written by the billing team. A contemporaneous time note is stronger than a reconstruction prepared for the study. Record the evidence type for each coded event so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a real variance."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across practice groups, fee sizes, and client types so one large matter does not define the result. Include matters with a supplement, a credit, and no adjustment at all. Draw the sample before reading the outcome so the selection is not biased toward the obvious overruns. If the population is small, review every matter and extend the period instead of lowering the evidence standard.","Stratify the sample by whether the quote recorded an assumed effort total. A matter with a detailed assumption can support a stronger comparison than a fee quoted as a single number. Report the results for each stratum rather than pooling them into one average. The reader should be able to see whether the finding depends on how the firm quotes its work."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each matter for documented scope change, estimate error, both, or neither, and add an unknown category for cases the evidence cannot resolve. Code the type of scope event, such as added document review, added hearing, client delay, or changed approval path. Keep a secondary code only when it changes the interpretation.","Have a second reviewer code a sample of matters and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the engagement letter and the client request, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that was applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as a scope change, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure variance as the difference between quoted effort and recorded effort, expressed in hours and as a percentage of the quote. Report the median and the range rather than a single average, because one very large matter can dominate a mean. Measure the timing of each scope event as well, since an early change is easier to price than one discovered near the invoice. Keep the raw records with the analysis file.","Do not convert the variance to a dollar figure without an approved internal rate and a clear statement that it is not a client charge. The study measures effort and scope, not price. If the firm wants a profitability view, run it as a separate analysis with the finance owner. A precise dollar number built on a notional rate is less useful than a clear hours comparison."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the share of matters with documented scope change, the share with estimate error, and the share with neither. Show the median variance for each group and the number of matters behind each figure. Compare only groups with similar scope and duration. A simple table is enough when the categories are defined and the counts are visible.","Write the main finding in plain language before presenting the coding detail. Include the matters that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. Show the scope events that were priced and the ones that were absorbed. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["Source records are often incomplete, and a missing supplement log is not proof that no scope change occurred. Report the completeness of the evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, changing client requirements, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A matter that was extended verbally may look like an estimate error until the client confirms the request.","Avoid comparing matters that differ in complexity or client responsiveness. A long matter has more opportunity for a scope event, and a fixed-fee matter with a narrow deliverable may have none by design. Describe the context beside the variance and avoid ranking individual timekeepers. The purpose is to improve the quote and the change process, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the sample, the quotes, and the time records in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, fee amounts, and matter details from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific amount. Keep the unredacted evidence separate from the working analysis.","Do not circulate a client-level or person-level list about flat-fee variance outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted client messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common cause. If documented scope change dominates, add a written change process and a supplement prompt to the matter plan. If estimate error dominates, revise the quoting assumptions for the practice group and test them on the next five matters. Do not launch both changes at once, because the result will be hard to interpret.","A lower variance is not the only useful outcome. Earlier scope conversations, clearer client expectations, and fewer invoice disputes can all improve the relationship even when the effort stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it shifts the conversation into an informal channel where it cannot be documented."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the study each quarter and after any major change to the quote template, the scope card, or the client's review process. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the matters that were excluded or still open beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the responsible attorneys and the finance owner, not only with the billing team. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which gap creates the most rework. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next study should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","url":"https://www.bls.gov/"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration","url":"https://www.sba.gov/"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/how-much-does-scope-change-explain-flat-fee-matter-variance.webp"},{"slug":"what-share-of-fee-application-exhibits-lack-a-matching-time-record","title":"What share of fee application exhibits lack a matching time record?","summary":"An exhibit audit that matches each fee application line to a contemporaneous time record and reports the gaps by task type and exhibit source.","cluster":"Court fees","finding":"A fee application can look complete while individual lines have no matching contemporaneous record, and those lines are the most likely to draw a court question.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks what share of fee application exhibits lack a matching time record. The unit is a claimed fee line in a court-appointed or fee-shifting application, and the measure is whether a contemporaneous source record supports the claimed date, task, and hours. The study covers the exhibit set filed with the application, not the firm's internal billing system alone. It does not evaluate whether the court should award the fee.","Scope the study to applications filed during the period in the selected jurisdiction. Include applications that were granted, reduced, and still pending so the denominator reflects the full workload. Exclude applications whose exhibits are sealed and unavailable to the study team, and record the number excluded. State the court, the case type, and the person who owns the file."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a matching time record as a contemporaneous entry or work product that supports the claimed date, the task description, and the hours within the firm's stated tolerance. A reconstructed summary prepared after the fact is not a matching record for this study. A calendar entry alone may support the date but not the task or the hours. Write two examples and one boundary case before coding.","Define a gap as a line with no matching record or with a record that supports only part of the claim. Keep the gap categories separate: no record, date mismatch, task mismatch, hours mismatch, and record created after the claim. A line can have more than one gap, so code the primary gap and note the secondary one. The separation shows which repair would help most."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every fee line claimed in the included applications. Report the number of applications, the number of lines, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. Count fee lines rather than pages, because a single page may contain several claims. If a line is grouped across tasks, state how it was treated in the coding guide.","Use the claimed line as the denominator and report the share with a matching record. Also report the share of claimed hours supported, because a line-level rate can hide a large unsupported block. Keep the two measures together in the report. A high line match rate with a low hours match rate is a different problem from a low line match rate."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the application, the time register, the contemporaneous notes, the work product, and the court's order as the primary evidence. Compare the exhibit line with the time system and with the source document, and record the date each was observed. If the work product is privileged or sealed, record the category and the reviewer who inspected it rather than copying the content. The evidence log should be detailed enough for a second reviewer to repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A contemporaneous time entry plus a dated work product is the strongest combination. A calendar entry or an email is weaker but can support part of the claim. A summary written for the application is the weakest source for a contemporaneous requirement. Record the source type for every line so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a record that never existed."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across task types, timekeepers, and application sizes so one large case does not define the result. Include research, document review, drafting, travel, and hearing time, because the evidence standard differs by task. Draw the sample before reading the court's outcome so the selection is not biased toward reduced applications. If the population is small, review every line and extend the period rather than lowering the evidence standard.","Stratify the sample by whether the line was supported by a work product or by a time entry alone. Report the gap rate for each stratum, because a task with no written product may need a different evidence rule. Keep the strata stable across periods so the comparison remains meaningful. The reader should be able to see whether the finding depends on the task mix."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each line as matched, partially matched, or unmatched, and record the gap type. Add an unknown category for lines the evidence cannot resolve. Code the source type, such as time entry, work product, calendar, email, or later summary, so the pattern is visible.","Have a second reviewer code a sample of lines and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the exhibit and the source record, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as contemporaneous, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure the line match rate, the hours match rate, and the distribution of gap types. Report the median and the range for the hours behind each line, because a few large blocks can dominate the total. Measure the age of the record at the time of the claim, since a note created months later is a different risk. Keep the raw counts with the analysis file so a reviewer can check the calculation.","Do not convert the unsupported hours to a dollar figure without the approved rate and a clear statement that the number is an estimate. The study measures evidentiary support, not the likely award. If a fee reduction is analyzed, keep it as a separate question with its own source and definition. A precise dollar estimate built on a contested rate is less useful than a clear gap count."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall match rate, the rate by task type, and the number of lines behind each figure. Show the share of hours supported and the most common gap type. Compare only applications with similar task mixes and evidence rules. Include the applications with no gaps so the report shows the full distribution rather than only the failures.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the lines that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["A missing record is not proof that the work did not happen, and a later summary is not proof that it did. Report the completeness of the evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, sealed exhibits, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A court may accept a reconstructed record in some circumstances, so the study should describe the standard it applied.","Avoid comparing applications that differ in task complexity or local rule. A jurisdiction that requires detailed time records will produce a different result from one that accepts block billing. Describe the court and the rule beside the rate and avoid ranking individual timekeepers. The purpose is to improve the exhibit file, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the applications, time records, and work products in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, case numbers, and privileged content from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the parties and the amount. Keep the sealed material in a separate restricted location.","Do not circulate a case-level or person-level list about exhibit gaps outside the management and legal team. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small sample cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the application and to quoted time entries. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common gap. If date mismatches dominate, add a date check to the exhibit index. If task mismatches dominate, improve the narrative standard and the time-entry prompt. If later summaries dominate, move the evidence collection into the week the work is performed. Test the change on a small set of lines before applying it to every application.","A higher match rate is not the only useful outcome. Faster assembly, fewer court questions, and a clearer review trail can all improve the process even when the rate stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it encourages timekeepers to write records after the fact just to satisfy a check."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the audit for each application cycle and after any change to the court's rule, the exhibit template, or the time system. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the applications that were excluded or still pending beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the responsible attorney and the billing owner, not only with the preparer. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which gap draws the most court questions. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next audit should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Courts, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/rules-policies/current-rules-practice-procedure/federal-rules-civil-procedure"},{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"United States Department of Labor work hours guidance","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/workhours"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/what-share-of-fee-application-exhibits-lack-a-matching-time-record.webp"},{"slug":"how-complete-is-the-audit-trail-for-written-off-time","title":"How complete is the audit trail for written-off time?","summary":"A completeness audit that checks whether each written-off time entry records the reason, approver, client basis, amount, and later outcome.","cluster":"Write-offs","finding":"A write-off can reduce a bill correctly and still leave no usable audit trail, which makes the next pricing and client conversation harder.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks how complete the audit trail is for time that was written off before or after billing. The unit is a written-off time entry or a write-off decision line, and the measure is whether the record contains the reason, the approver, the client basis, the amount, and the final outcome. The study looks at the documentation, not at whether the write-off was commercially wise. It does not attempt to reconstruct a decision that was never recorded.","Scope the study to matters with a write-off posted during the period. Include write-offs made at prebill, at invoice review, and after a client dispute. Exclude corrections that were purely clerical, such as a duplicate entry reversal, and record the number excluded. State the billing system, the approval policy, and the owner of the analysis."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a complete write-off record as one that links the amount, the affected time entries, the reason, the approver, and the client or matter basis. A note that says only courtesy or client request is not complete for this study. A complete record should let a reviewer understand what was reduced and why without interviewing the person who approved it. Write two examples and one boundary case before coding.","Distinguish a write-off from a write-down, a credit, and a fee adjustment. A write-off removes time that will not be billed, while a write-down reduces the rate or the amount on a billed line. Code the type separately because the approval path and the client communication differ. A blended category would hide which control failed."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every write-off decision posted in the period, counted at the decision-line level. Report the number of matters, the number of lines, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. If one decision covers several time entries, state whether it is counted once or per entry. Use the same unit across periods so the completeness rate is comparable.","Report the share of write-off value covered by a complete record as well as the share of lines. A small number of large write-offs can carry most of the value, so a line count alone may overstate the quality of the trail. Keep both measures in the report. The value measure should use the posted amount, not a notional rate."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the write-off approval, the billing system note, the client correspondence, and the invoice or credit memo as the primary evidence. Compare the posted amount with the time entries it covers and record the date each source was observed. If the approval is only in an email, record the email and the date rather than inferring the decision. The evidence log should let a second reviewer repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A signed approval with a stated reason is stronger than a system note added later. A client email that explains the dispute is stronger than an internal summary of it. Record the evidence type for each line so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a missing decision."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across matter types, approvers, and write-off sizes so one large matter does not define the result. Include write-offs with a client dispute, a prebill adjustment, and an internal quality decision. Draw the sample before reading the outcome so the selection is not biased toward the well-documented cases. If the population is small, review every line and extend the period.","Stratify the sample by whether the write-off was above or below the firm's approval threshold. A small adjustment may follow a lighter process by design, while a large one should carry a fuller record. Report the completeness rate for each stratum rather than pooling them. The reader should be able to see whether the policy is being followed at each level."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each required field as present, partial, or missing, and record the write-off type. Add an unknown category for records the evidence cannot resolve. Code the reason category, such as client dispute, quality correction, relationship decision, scope confusion, or billing error, because the reason shapes the fix.","Have a second reviewer code a sample and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the approval and the client correspondence, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as a complete record, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure the completeness rate by line, by value, and by required field. Report the median and the range for the amount behind each line so a few large write-offs are visible. Measure the time between the write-off decision and the invoice or credit memo, because a late record is harder to explain. Keep the raw counts with the analysis file so a reviewer can check the calculation.","Do not convert the written-off amount to a realization percentage without the approved fee and collection data. The study measures documentation, not profitability. If the firm wants a realization view, run it as a separate analysis with the finance owner. A precise percentage built on incomplete data is less useful than a clear field-level gap count."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall completeness rate, the field-level gaps, and the number of lines behind each figure. Show the share of value covered by a complete record and the most common reason category. Compare only groups with similar approval rules and matter types. Include the records that are complete so the report shows the full distribution.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the cases that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["A missing note is not proof that no reason existed, and a later summary is not proof that the reason was considered at the time. Report the completeness of the evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, changing approval rules, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A write-off approved in a meeting may have no system record even when the decision was sound.","Avoid comparing groups that differ in client sensitivity or approval authority. A relationship write-off may be deliberately documented in a narrower channel than a quality correction. Describe the context beside the rate and avoid ranking individual approvers. The purpose is to improve the trail, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the approvals, client correspondence, and billing records in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, amounts, and matter details from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific amount. Keep privileged material in a separate restricted location.","Do not circulate a client-level or approver-level list about write-offs outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted client messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common gap. If the reason field is weak, add a short required prompt with a small code list. If the approval is missing, route the write-off through the system rather than email. If the client basis is absent, add a link to the correspondence. Test the change on one approval level before applying it broadly.","A higher completeness rate is not the only useful outcome. Faster approvals, clearer client conversations, and fewer repeated write-offs can all improve the process even when the rate stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it turns the reason field into a longer free-text box that no one reads."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the audit each billing cycle and after any change to the approval policy, the billing system, or the client's fee arrangement. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the lines that were excluded or still open beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the billing manager and the finance owner, not only with the preparer. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which gap causes the most rework. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next audit should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission data security guidance","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/data-security"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","url":"https://www.bls.gov/"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/how-complete-is-the-audit-trail-for-written-off-time.webp"},{"slug":"how-often-do-rate-cards-and-matter-records-disagree-at-invoice-time","title":"How often do rate cards and matter records disagree at invoice time?","summary":"A reconciliation study that compares each invoice rate with the approved rate card, the matter record, and the client's written rate approval.","cluster":"Rate management","finding":"Rate disagreements at invoice time usually come from a stale matter record or a missed client approval rather than from an arithmetic error on the invoice.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks how often the rate used on an invoice disagrees with the approved rate card and the matter record. The unit is a billed time line, and the measure is whether the rate, the timekeeper, and the effective date match the client's written approval. The study covers invoices issued during the period, not the rate negotiation itself. It does not evaluate whether the approved rate is competitive.","Scope the study to matters with at least one timekeeper whose rate changed during the engagement or during the prior twelve months. Include invoices that were paid, adjusted, and still open so the denominator reflects the full workload. Exclude lines with no timekeeper rate, such as flat-fee or cost-only lines, and record the number excluded. State the billing system, the rate card source, and the owner of the analysis."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a rate disagreement as a billed line where the rate, the timekeeper identity, or the effective date does not match the approved rate card and the client's written approval. A line with the right rate but the wrong timekeeper is still a disagreement for this study. A line with a correct rate that was approved orally is a documentation gap, not a rate error. Write two examples and one boundary case before coding.","Define a stale matter record as a matter where the rate field was not updated after an approved change. Keep it separate from a missing client approval and from a keying error. A matter can have more than one cause, so code the primary cause and note the secondary one. The separation shows whether the fix belongs to the rate process, the matter setup, or the invoice review."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every billed time line on the included invoices. Report the number of invoices, the number of lines, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. Count lines rather than invoices, because one invoice can contain several timekeepers and rates. If a line is billed at a blended rate, state how it was treated in the coding guide.","Use the billed line as the denominator and report the disagreement rate by line and by billed value. A small number of high-value lines can carry most of the exposure, so the value measure matters. Keep both measures in the report. The value measure should use the billed amount, not a notional rate."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the invoice, the approved rate card, the matter record, and the client's written rate approval as the primary evidence. Compare the rate and the effective date across all four sources and record the date each was observed. If the matter record and the rate card disagree, do not assume that one is correct without the client approval. The evidence log should let a second reviewer repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A signed client approval is stronger than an internal rate card, and a current rate card is stronger than a matter record that was never updated. A billing system note is weaker than a dated approval. Record the evidence type for each line so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a rate error."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across clients, practice groups, and timekeeper levels so one rate change does not define the result. Include lines with a recent increase, a client-specific exception, and no change at all. Draw the sample before reading the invoice outcome so the selection is not biased toward the disputed lines. If the population is small, review every line and extend the period.","Stratify the sample by whether the rate change had a written client approval on file. A change with a clear approval tests the matter record and the invoice, while a change without one tests the approval process. Report the disagreement rate for each stratum rather than pooling them. The reader should be able to see where the control breaks."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each line as matched, stale matter record, missing client approval, wrong timekeeper, wrong effective date, keying error, or unknown. Add a note for the rate difference and the date it was observed. Keep the code list short enough that two reviewers can apply it consistently.","Have a second reviewer code a sample and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the rate card and the client approval, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on which source is authoritative, stop and fix the hierarchy before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure the disagreement rate by line, by billed value, and by cause. Report the median and the range for the rate difference so a few large matters are visible. Measure the time between the approved effective date and the first invoice that used the new rate. Keep the raw counts with the analysis file so a reviewer can check the calculation.","Do not convert the rate difference to a revenue figure without the approved fee and collection data. The study measures rate accuracy, not profitability. If the firm wants a revenue view, run it as a separate analysis with the finance owner. A precise dollar figure built on an unapproved rate is less useful than a clear mismatch count."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall disagreement rate, the rate by cause, and the number of lines behind each figure. Show the share of billed value affected and the most common stale-record pattern. Compare only groups with similar rate structures and approval rules. Include the lines that matched so the report shows the full distribution.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the cases that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["A missing approval is not proof that no approval existed, and an updated rate card is not proof that the client agreed. Report the completeness of the evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, mid-period changes, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A rate approved in a call may be correct but undocumented, which is a different problem from a wrong rate.","Avoid comparing clients with different rate structures or effective-date rules. A client with a fiscal-year rate cycle will produce a different pattern from one with a calendar-year cycle. Describe the context beside the rate and avoid ranking individual billers. The purpose is to improve the rate record, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the invoices, rate cards, and client approvals in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, rates, and matter details from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific rate. Keep the unredacted evidence separate from the working analysis.","Do not circulate a client-level or timekeeper-level list about rate disagreements outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted client messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common cause. If the matter record is stale, add a rate-change task to the matter setup checklist with a named owner and an effective date. If the client approval is missing, add a required field before the rate can be saved. If keying errors dominate, add a second review at the first invoice after a change. Test one change before adding another.","A lower disagreement rate is not the only useful outcome. Faster invoice review and fewer client questions can improve the process even when the rate stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it adds a step that the team works around instead of using."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the study each quarter and after any rate cycle or matter-system change. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the lines that were excluded or still open beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the billing manager and the rate owner, not only with the preparer. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which gap causes the most client questions. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next study should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","url":"https://www.bls.gov/"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau","url":"https://www.census.gov/"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/how-often-do-rate-cards-and-matter-records-disagree-at-invoice-time.webp"},{"slug":"how-much-invoice-review-time-goes-to-duplicate-or-split-cost-lines","title":"How much invoice review time goes to duplicate or split cost lines?","summary":"A time study that measures the review minutes spent identifying duplicate, split, and unsupported cost lines before a client invoice is released.","cluster":"Disbursements","finding":"Duplicate and split cost lines consume review time that is rarely counted, and the pattern usually points to a vendor or intake control rather than to the reviewer.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks how much invoice review time goes to duplicate, split, and unsupported cost lines. The unit is a review minute attached to a cost line, and the measure is the active time spent identifying and resolving the issue before the invoice is released. The study covers the cost review stage, not the vendor payment process. It does not attempt to estimate the total cost of the billing function.","Scope the study to invoices with at least one cost line reviewed during the period. Include invoices that were released, held, and adjusted so the denominator reflects the full workload. Exclude invoices with no cost lines, and record the number excluded. State the billing system, the cost ledger, and the person who owns the time log."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a duplicate as a cost line that repeats an amount already posted for the same matter, vendor, and period. Define a split as a single vendor charge divided across invoices or lines in a way that obscures the original transaction. Define unsupported as a cost with no receipt, no matter reference, or no client authorization. Write two examples and one boundary case so reviewers apply the same rule.","Distinguish a legitimate multiple charge from a duplicate. Two court reporter invoices for two separate hearings are not duplicates even when the amount is identical. A monthly courier summary that lists several deliveries is not a split merely because it has several lines. Code the evidence that shows the charge is separate, not just the similarity of the amount."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every cost line reviewed in the period, counted once per invoice. Report the number of invoices, the number of cost lines, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. If a line was reviewed more than once, count the active minutes from each pass but keep the line as one unit. State how rework was handled in the coding guide.","Report the review minutes per cost line and the share of minutes spent on flagged lines. A high share can reflect a small number of complex invoices, so report the number of invoices behind the share. Keep the raw time entries with the analysis file. The denominator should include the lines that were cleared quickly, not only the difficult ones."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the cost ledger, the vendor invoice, the client invoice, and the reviewer's time log as the primary evidence. Compare the line with the ledger and the original invoice, and record the date each was observed. If the time log is missing, mark the line as incomplete instead of estimating from memory. The evidence log should let a second reviewer repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A contemporaneous time log is stronger than a later estimate, and a vendor invoice is stronger than a summary line in the billing system. A client authorization is stronger than an internal assumption. Record the evidence type for each line so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a real review burden."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across vendors, cost types, and invoice sizes so one large invoice does not define the result. Include lines that were cleared without a flag, because the review time for a clean line is part of the baseline. Draw the sample before reading the outcome so the selection is not biased toward the difficult cases. If the population is small, review every line and extend the period.","Stratify the sample by whether the cost was received through a vendor statement or a per-matter invoice. A monthly statement may require more allocation work and more duplicate checks. Report the minutes per line for each stratum rather than pooling them. The reader should be able to see where the review burden actually sits."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each flagged line as duplicate, split, unsupported, rate variance, or other, and record the stage where it was found. Add an unknown category for lines the evidence cannot resolve. Code the outcome as cleared, adjusted, or escalated so the review time can be interpreted.","Have a second reviewer code a sample of flagged lines and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the ledger and the vendor invoice, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as a duplicate, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure active review minutes with a short log completed at the moment of the work. Separate the time spent searching, comparing, deciding, and communicating so the largest component is visible. Report the median and the range rather than a single average, because one complex invoice can dominate a mean. Keep the raw entries with the analysis file so a reviewer can check the calculation.","Do not convert the minutes to a dollar figure without an approved labor rate and a clear statement that it is an internal estimate. The study measures effort, not price. If the firm wants a cost view, run it separately with the finance owner. A precise number built on rounded guesses is less useful than a range with a clear source."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall minutes per cost line, the share spent on flagged lines, and the number of invoices behind each figure. Show the median and range for each flag type and the most common stage where the issue was found. Compare only groups with similar vendor and cost mixes. Include the clean lines so the report shows the full distribution.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the cases that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["Self-reported time can be rounded, and a missing log is not proof that no review occurred. Report the completeness of the time evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, changing vendor arrangements, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A line that was cleared quickly may still have required a check that the reviewer did not log.","Avoid comparing invoices that differ in cost volume or client requirements. A matter with many small costs will produce a different review pattern from one with a single expert fee. Describe the context beside the minutes and avoid ranking individual reviewers. The purpose is to improve the intake and the queue, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the invoices, cost ledger, and time logs in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, amounts, and vendor details from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific amount. Keep the unredacted evidence separate from the working analysis.","Do not circulate a person-level or client-level list about review time outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted vendor messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common flag. If duplicates dominate, add a vendor invoice number check at intake. If splits dominate, require the original invoice to be attached before allocation. If unsupported costs dominate, add a receipt and authorization prompt to the cost template. Test the change on one vendor before applying it broadly.","A lower review time is not the only useful outcome. Fewer client questions and a cleaner cost ledger can improve the process even when the minutes stay similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it pushes the check into a private channel where it cannot be reviewed."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the study each invoice cycle and after any change to the vendor mix, the cost template, or the billing system. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the lines that were excluded or still open beside the main measure. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the billing manager and the cost ledger owner, not only with the reviewer. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which flag creates the most rework. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next study should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission data security guidance","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/data-security"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","url":"https://www.bls.gov/"},{"label":"United States Department of Labor work hours guidance","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/workhours"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/how-much-invoice-review-time-goes-to-duplicate-or-split-cost-lines.webp"},{"slug":"how-complete-are-matter-handoff-records-when-the-billing-owner-changes-role","title":"How complete are matter handoff records when the billing owner changes role?","summary":"A completeness audit that checks whether a billing owner handoff records open items, owners, deadlines, evidence, and system access.","cluster":"Role design","finding":"A billing owner can change role without the matter losing its legal owner, yet the handoff record often omits the open items and access steps that keep the invoice moving.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks how complete the handoff record is when the billing owner for a matter changes role. The unit is a matter handoff, and the measure is whether the record contains the open items, the new owner, the deadlines, the evidence location, and the access confirmation. The study covers role changes, not temporary absences. It does not evaluate the departing person's performance.","Scope the study to matters with a billing owner change during the period, whether the change was a promotion, a transfer, a departure, or a reorganization. Include matters that were active, on hold, and closing so the denominator reflects the full workload. Exclude matters with no billing owner field, and record the number excluded. State the billing system, the document system, and the owner of the analysis."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define a complete handoff as one that names the new owner, lists the open time and cost items, records the next deadline, points to the evidence, and confirms the access change. A note that says please take over this matter is not complete for this study. A complete record should let the new owner act without interviewing the person who left. Write two examples and one boundary case before coding.","Distinguish a handoff from a reassignment in the system. A reassignment changes the owner field but may not transfer context, deadlines, or access. A handoff includes the written context and the confirmation that the new owner can act. Code the two separately because a system reassignment alone can look complete while the work stalls."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every matter with a billing owner change in the period, counted once per change. Report the number of matters, the number of changes, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. If a matter changed owners twice, count each change as a separate handoff or state the rule used. Keep the unit stable across periods so the completeness rate is comparable.","Report the completeness rate by matter and by open item. A matter can have a complete header but an incomplete list of open costs, so the item-level measure matters. Keep both measures in the report. The item measure should count only the items that existed at the time of the handoff."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the handoff note, the matter record, the billing queue, the access log, and the new owner's confirmation as the primary evidence. Compare the open items in the note with the items actually open in the system and record the date each was observed. If the handoff note is missing, mark the matter as incomplete rather than inferring the content from later emails. The evidence log should let a second reviewer repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A contemporaneous handoff note is stronger than a later summary, and the system queue is stronger than a memory of what was open. An access confirmation is stronger than an assumption that the new owner can log in. Record the evidence type for each item so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from an access failure."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across practice groups, offices, and handoff types so one reorganization does not define the result. Include planned and unplanned changes, because the available notice differs. Draw the sample before reading the outcome so the selection is not biased toward the well-documented cases. If the population is small, review every change and extend the period.","Stratify the sample by whether the handoff was planned at least two weeks in advance. A planned change should support a fuller record and a tested access transition. Report the completeness rate for each stratum rather than pooling them. The reader should be able to see whether the process depends on notice."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code each required element as present, partial, or missing, and record the handoff type. Add an unknown category for records the evidence cannot resolve. Code the open item type, such as unbilled time, unposted cost, client question, rejection, or deadline, so the pattern is visible.","Have a second reviewer code a sample and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the handoff note and the system queue, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as an open item, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure the completeness rate by matter, by element, and by open item. Report the median and the range for the number of open items per handoff so a few complex matters are visible. Measure the days between the owner change and the new owner's first action, because a complete note that no one uses is not a working handoff. Keep the raw counts with the analysis file.","Do not convert the delay to a dollar figure without an approved labor rate and a clear statement that it is an internal estimate. The study measures documentation and readiness, not price. If the firm wants a cost view, run it separately with the finance owner. A precise number built on incomplete records is less useful than a clear element-level gap count."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall completeness rate, the element-level gaps, and the number of matters behind each figure. Show the median days to first action and the most common missing element. Compare only groups with similar handoff notice and matter complexity. Include the handoffs that were complete so the report shows the full distribution.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the cases that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["A missing note is not proof that no handoff conversation occurred, and a complete note is not proof that the new owner could act. Report the completeness of the evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, concurrent system changes, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. A handoff during a reorganization may be documented in a channel the study does not capture.","Avoid comparing handoffs that differ in notice, matter complexity, or client sensitivity. A planned transfer with a long runway should look different from a sudden departure. Describe the context beside the rate and avoid ranking individual owners. The purpose is to improve the handoff process, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the handoff notes, matter records, and access logs in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, matter details, and personal information from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific item. Keep the unredacted evidence separate from the working analysis.","Do not circulate a person-level or matter-level list about handoff quality outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted handoff messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common gap. If open items are missing, add a required checklist to the handoff template. If access is unconfirmed, make the access test a step before the change is marked complete. If the new owner's first action is late, add a named backup and a start date. Test one change on the next handoff before applying it broadly.","A higher completeness rate is not the only useful outcome. Faster first action and fewer client questions can improve the process even when the rate stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it becomes a long form that the team fills in after the fact."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the audit each quarter and after any reorganization, system change, or role redesign. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the handoffs that were excluded or still open beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the billing manager and the new owners, not only with the departing team. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which gap causes the most delay. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next audit should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission data security guidance","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/data-security"},{"label":"United States Department of Labor work hours guidance","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/workhours"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/how-complete-are-matter-handoff-records-when-the-billing-owner-changes-role.webp"},{"slug":"what-share-of-invoice-inquiries-close-without-a-fee-adjustment","title":"What share of invoice inquiries close without a fee adjustment?","summary":"An outcome study that follows each client invoice inquiry to closure and separates explanation-only resolutions from credits, write-downs, and rework.","cluster":"Invoice follow-up","finding":"Many invoice inquiries are resolved by explaining the record rather than changing the fee, but the explanation work is rarely measured and often lands on the same reviewer twice.","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This protocol asks what share of client invoice inquiries close without a fee adjustment. The unit is an inquiry about an issued invoice, and the measure is the closure outcome: explanation only, credit, write-down, rework, or still open. The study follows the inquiry from receipt to closure, not just the first response. It does not evaluate whether the client's question was reasonable or whether the invoice was correct.","Scope the study to invoices issued during the period that received at least one inquiry. Include inquiries that arrived before the due date, after the due date, and after payment. Exclude routine reminder messages that did not ask a question, and record the number excluded. State the billing system, the client communication channel, and the owner of the analysis."]},{"heading":"Defining the event","paragraphs":["Define an inquiry as a client message that questions an amount, a time entry, a cost, a rate, or the invoice format and expects a response. A request for a copy of the invoice is an administrative request, not an inquiry for this study. A message that combines an inquiry with a payment instruction counts as an inquiry. Write two examples and one boundary case before coding.","Define closure as the point when the client confirms the resolution or the firm records a final adjustment and no further response is pending. An explanation that the client never acknowledges is not closed for this study. Keep still-open inquiries in a separate category so the denominator reflects the full workload. A reopened inquiry after closure should be coded as a new event with a link to the original."]},{"heading":"Population and denominator","paragraphs":["The population is every inquiry received on an included invoice during the period. Report the number of invoices, the number of inquiries, the number excluded, and the number in the final sample. If one invoice received several inquiries, count each one separately or state the rule used. Keep the unit stable across periods so the closure rate is comparable.","Report the share closed without an adjustment, the share closed with an adjustment, and the share still open. Also report the share that required rework even when the fee did not change, such as a corrected narrative or a resubmitted cost receipt. A pure explanation and a silent correction are different outcomes, so keep them apart. Report the adjustment value beside the count so a small number of large credits cannot hide behind a high explanation rate."]},{"heading":"Evidence sources","paragraphs":["Use the client message, the invoice, the time and cost records, and the final response as the primary evidence. Compare the question with the invoice line and record the date each was observed. If the client's confirmation is missing, mark the inquiry as unconfirmed rather than assuming closure. The evidence log should let a second reviewer repeat the match.","Rank the sources so reviewers know what to trust first. A written client message is stronger than an internal summary of a call, and the invoice record is stronger than a later recollection. A credit memo or adjustment is stronger than a verbal promise. Record the evidence type for each inquiry so the analysis can separate a documentation gap from a real outcome."]},{"heading":"Sampling","paragraphs":["Sample across clients, invoice sizes, and inquiry types so one large dispute does not define the result. Include inquiries about time, costs, rates, and format, because the resolution path differs. Draw the sample before reading the outcome so the selection is not biased toward the easy closures. If the population is small, review every inquiry and extend the period.","Stratify the sample by whether the inquiry arrived before or after the due date. A late inquiry may receive a different response because the payment clock is already running. Report the closure rate and the adjustment share for each stratum rather than pooling them. The reader should be able to see whether timing changes the outcome."]},{"heading":"Coding the record","paragraphs":["Use a short code list that points to a specific repair. Code the outcome as explanation only, credit, write-down, rework, escalation, or still open, and record the inquiry type. Add an unknown category for cases the evidence cannot resolve. Code the number of touches and the owner of each response so the effort is visible.","Have a second reviewer code a sample and compare the results. Resolve disagreements by returning to the client message and the invoice record, not by majority vote. Report the agreement rate and revise any code that is applied inconsistently. If the reviewers cannot agree on what counts as a fee adjustment, stop and fix the definition before continuing."]},{"heading":"Measuring the variable","paragraphs":["Measure the time from inquiry to first response and from inquiry to closure, using a short log completed during the work. Report the median and the range rather than a single average, because one complex dispute can dominate a mean. Measure the number of touches and the amount of any adjustment separately. Keep the raw entries with the analysis file so a reviewer can check the calculation.","Do not convert the response time to a dollar figure without an approved labor rate and a clear statement that it is an internal estimate. The study measures effort and outcome, not price. If the firm wants a cost view, run it separately with the finance owner. A precise number built on rounded guesses is less useful than a range with a clear source."]},{"heading":"Analysis and reporting","paragraphs":["Report the overall closure rate, the adjustment share, and the median response time. Show the results by inquiry type and by timing, with the number of inquiries behind each figure. Compare only groups with similar clients and invoice sizes. Include the inquiries that closed without any change so the report shows the full distribution.","Give the reader one table that answers the main question and put the coding detail in an appendix. Write the finding in plain language before presenting the technical definition. Include the cases that contradict the pattern rather than hiding them in an average. A report that cannot be acted on is just another record of the past."]},{"heading":"Limits and sources of error","paragraphs":["A client may accept an explanation without saying so, and a missing confirmation is not proof that the inquiry stayed open. Report the completeness of the closure evidence before drawing a conclusion. Small samples, mixed communication channels, and reviewer disagreement can all limit interpretation. An adjustment made outside the billing system may not appear in the record.","Avoid comparing inquiries that differ in complexity or relationship history. A long-standing client may receive a faster explanation than a new client with the same question. Describe the context beside the rate and avoid ranking individual reviewers. The purpose is to improve the response process, not to judge professional worth."]},{"heading":"Privacy and retention","paragraphs":["Store the client messages, invoices, and adjustment records in a controlled folder with access limited to the study team. Remove client names, amounts, and matter details from the analysis file when they are not needed. If an example is shared, use a role label and redact the client and the specific amount. Keep the unredacted evidence separate from the working analysis.","Do not circulate a client-level or reviewer-level list about inquiry outcomes outside the management context. Aggregate the results before sharing them more broadly, and check that a small group cannot be re-identified. The same rule applies to screenshots of the billing system and to quoted client messages. Privacy is part of the method, not an afterthought added at the end."]},{"heading":"Applying the result","paragraphs":["Choose one change that matches the most common inquiry type. If time narratives dominate, improve the entry standard and the prebill sample. If costs dominate, add a receipt check before the invoice is released. If format questions dominate, fix the invoice template. Test one change on the next invoice cycle before expanding it.","A higher explanation-only share is not the only useful outcome. Faster closure, fewer repeated questions, and a clearer record can improve the relationship even when the adjustment share stays similar. Report the direct result and the service result separately. Stop the change if it shifts the conversation into an informal channel where it cannot be reviewed."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Repeat the study each invoice cycle and after any change to the invoice template, the client contact, or the billing system. Keep the definitions and the unit stable so periods can be compared. Report the inquiries that were excluded or still open beside the main rate. Review the coding guide after the first repetition and keep the changes visible.","Review the result with the billing manager and the client-facing owner, not only with the responder. Ask which fix is easiest to sustain and which inquiry type creates the most rework. Set one change and a follow-up date, then check whether the change held. The next study should test the repair, not just repeat the same count."]}],"sources":[{"label":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"},{"label":"Legal Electronic Data Exchange Standard","url":"https://ledes.org/"},{"label":"U.S. Government Accountability Office Green Book","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service recordkeeping guidance","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission data security guidance","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/data-security"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","url":"https://www.bls.gov/"}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-10","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-10/what-share-of-invoice-inquiries-close-without-a-fee-adjustment.webp"}];
